The  driver  supports Microsoft’s Windows Sound System, the MAD16
chip based hardware, and their clones.  The base I/O port is  set
by  a jumper on the board; valid choices are 0x530, 0x604, 0xE80,
or 0xF40.  Both IRQ and DMA channels are  software  programmable.
The IRQ may be set to 7, 9, 10, or 11, and the DMA channel may be
set  to  0, 1, or 3.  The device must have bit 0 (value 1) set to
enable the MAD16 support.  This is to avoid  potential  conflicts
with other devices when probing the MAD16 because it requires use
of  extra  I/O  ports  not in the base port range.  Chips needing
this flag include the OPTi 82C928, 82C929, 82C831,  and  the  OAK
OTI‐601D.   Setting bit 1 (value 2) in disables the joystick port
on MAD16 hardware.
